          <h2>A commuter suburb with a professional core</h2>
          <p>Edgbaston is best known for the Five Ways office cluster, the University of Birmingham campus and the Queen Elizabeth Hospital, alongside its role as one of the city's more established residential areas close to the centre. That combination shapes the kind of breakdown we see here: a good proportion happen either at the start or end of the working day, as vehicles that have sat overnight or all day are asked to start again, or as commuters head out onto the Hagley Road into slower-moving traffic.</p>

          <h2>Hospital and university recovery</h2>
          <p>If you break down at the Queen Elizabeth Hospital, the university campus, or one of Edgbaston's office sites, the layout can involve multiple car parks, service roads and building entrances, so the site name and building number help us a great deal. These call-outs often need to be handled with minimum fuss — we treat hospital and campus recovery the same way whether it's one car in a quiet corner of the car park or a vehicle blocking a thoroughfare.</p>

          <h2>The Hagley Road out towards Quinton and Halesowen</h2>
          <p>Edgbaston also sits on the A456 Hagley Road, the route many drivers use heading further out towards Quinton, Halesowen and the western edge of the conurbation. If your breakdown happens on this stretch rather than close to the centre, let us know your direction of travel and any nearby junctions or landmarks — the road carries a steady flow of commuter traffic in both directions, which affects how we plan the recovery.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Commuting into or out of Edgbaston?</strong> A flat battery after a car has sat in a hospital or office car park all day is one of the most common calls we get here. If your car won't start at the end of the working day, get yourself somewhere safe and give us a call — we'll talk through what's happened and get moving.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

